# The General Court lane, one step per line, run in order by watchers/gc_lane.py.
# A step is what follows "python3". "handover " in front means the step takes
# archive/.lock itself. A line is run once; logs/gc_lane.done lists what has run.
# Add a step only once its fetcher has been read for the one-worker rules.

# 2015-2016: the 883 bills the database's Docket view does not reach. Stopped
# at 09:51 on the 11th, 102 pages in, when a review found this fetcher cached
# the firewall's block page and missed a reset wrapped in a URLError; the step
# below supersedes it, and the 102 pages are cached.
# fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2015-2016 --seed Docket_db_2015-2016.txt --delay 15 --stop-refused 2

# 2015-2016, with the seed corrected. The database's "2016" is not 2016: its
# 1,962 SessionYear-2016 rows are all dated 2015, the first-year history of
# 190 bills carried over, and two of them carry another bill's LSR. This seed
# (Docket_db_2015.txt) keeps only session-2015 rows of bills data/bills.json
# files under 2015, with the bill's own LSR: 8,322 rows on 716 bills. Every
# one of the 1,072 bills filed under 2016 comes from its own page; those
# already cached are not asked again.
# Stopped at 11:36 on the 11th, 381 of the 1,072 cached, on two 60-second read
# timeouts 97 minutes in -- the second run in a row to slow or stop about
# ninety minutes into business hours. netcheck at 12:37 found the address
# answering as it always has. Resumed at 25s apart rather than 15.
# fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2015-2016 --seed Docket_db_2015.txt --delay 15 --stop-refused 2
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2015-2016 --seed Docket_db_2015.txt --delay 25 --stop-refused 2
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-docket

# EMPTY DOCKET PAGES, asked again. 17 cached pages of 2017-2022 carry the
# bill's title and no docket row; a person opened SB 68 of 2017 on the 11th
# and saw twelve rows ending in its signature and chapter 35. With
# --refetch-empty a run asks for those pages and for any bill never cached
# (12 more), and nothing else: about 29 requests across the three terms.
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2017-2018 --delay 25 --stop-refused 2 --refetch-empty
watchers/rest.py 600 after-refetch-2017-2018
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2019-2020 --delay 25 --stop-refused 2 --refetch-empty
watchers/rest.py 600 after-refetch-2019-2020
fetch_archive_docket.py --term 2021-2022 --delay 25 --stop-refused 2 --refetch-empty
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-refetch-2021-2022

# BILL TEXT, 2024 BACK TO 1989, newest first -- the person's order of the
# 11th: the modern terms are the useful ones, and all of it eventually.
# --plan: 30,049 requests for bills and constitutional amendments, 954
# more for resolutions and the rest. Runs of 800 at 20s apart, an hour's
# rest between; each run skips what is on disk, so the same range queued
# again carries on where the last stopped.
#
# A short run first on 2024: the application's path (stored id with sy),
# so the LSR check meets its pages before it meets 800 of them.
# Superseded, in the old oldest-first order:
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2021 --to 2021 --kinds HB,SB --budget 20 --note proving
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2017 --to 2021 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --budget 800 --note 2017-21-a
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2023 --to 2024 --budget 25 --note 2023-24-proving
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2023 --to 2024 --budget 800 --note 2023-24-a
# fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2017 --to 2021 --kinds HR,SR,HCR,SCR,HJR,SJR --budget 400 --note 2017-21-resolutions
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2024 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB --delay 20 --budget 25 --note proving-2024
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-proving-2024
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-01
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-01

# SPONSORS, 1989-2022: THE PROVING RUN, AND IT IS THIRTEEN REQUESTS.
# Seventeen of nineteen terms have no sponsor at all -- data/sponsors.json
# holds 2023-2024 and 2025-2026 and nothing else -- and byAnyMember.aspx
# answers per legislator rather than per bill, so the whole archive is a few
# thousand requests instead of thirty thousand. The person asked for this on
# the 11th, after the bill text and alongside it.
#
# NO PAGE FROM THAT ENDPOINT IS ON THIS DISK. Every regex in the fetcher was
# written from markup read during a survey, so the parser is a hypothesis and
# a sweep on a hypothesis is a sweep on a guess. These two steps are the
# experiment instead:
#   --members   one GET: the real member list, the real hidden fields, saved
#               as sponsors_form.html for a person to read.
#   --probe 6   six legislators, both modes, 12 requests, SCORED against
#               data/sponsors.json -- which came from the General Court's own
#               database and not from this script. A member the database says
#               prime-sponsored HB1234 in 2025 must come back with HB1234 in
#               their Prime list. That one comparison tests four things no
#               amount of reading the HTML can settle: whether the row parser
#               finds rows, whether prime is radio 0 or radio 1, whether this
#               list's member ids are the database's ids, and whether a
#               member's list really does reach back across terms.
# The sweep proper is not queued: it waits for that score to be read.
fetch_sponsors_by_member.py --members
watchers/rest.py 600 after-sponsor-roster
fetch_sponsors_by_member.py --probe 6 --delay 20
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-sponsor-probe
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-02
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-02

# FINISH 2023 BEFORE PIVOTING, on the person's condition of the 12th: "if bill
# text is retrieved going back to 2023 ... we're fine to pivot to the Senate
# calendar proving run and other quicker fetches".
#
# 2024 is complete, 1,300 of 1,300. 2023 is 470 of 638 because bills-02 spent
# most of its 800 finishing 2024. 168 requests, under an hour, and it closes
# the whole 2023-2024 term rather than leaving it three-quarters done -- which
# matters because a term is the unit a reader browses in.
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 2023 --to 2023 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --delay 20 --budget 200 --note finish-2023
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-finish-2023

# THE SENATE CALENDARS OF 1998-2008, asked for by the person on the 12th.
# 664 of them are still marked "wanted" in archive/queue.csv from 9 September,
# each with "HTTP Error 403: Forbidden" against it, and that 403 is the reason
# the drain stopped. 2008-2026 are already on disk -- 984 PDFs, each with its
# text beside it -- so this is the ten years before them, plus fourteen
# stragglers of 2008.
#
# They are the only source for the Senate's committee reports of 2008-2024 and
# for the governor's veto messages on Senate bills, which are printed in the
# chamber the bill came from.
#
# THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS BLOCK STOPPED THE LANE, on the 12th at 13:29, and
# the lane sat idle from then until the evening with 102 steps behind it.
# It asked fetch_senate_calendars.py for 2007, which read the index, found no
# document whose LABEL carried "2007", said "0 calendars listed" and exited 1.
# The year IS offered -- the script prints something else when it is not --
# so the older editions' labels simply do not name their year, and the filter
# at fetch_senate_calendars.py:126 threw all 61 away. A deterministic failure,
# not a refusal, and the lane stops on any non-zero exit.
#
# The names were already on this disk. archive/queue.csv holds all 664, each
# with the exact filename the index listed on 9 September, and the drain that
# reads that queue never constructs or searches for anything. So the queue is
# drained instead, by fetch_calendar_archive.py -- which could not run in the
# lane either until the 12th: it kept archive/.lock by hand, exited at once
# when the lock was fresh, and deleted the lock when it finished. It now takes
# the lock through refusal.hold() like the others, reads every answer through
# refusal.classify(), stops on ONE refusal, refuses to save anything that is
# not a PDF, and writes atomically (preflight: "the calendar drain runs under
# the lane and leaves its lock alone").
#
# Each step is a different year range because the lane knows a step by its
# text, and five identical lines would be done after the first. Newest first.
#
# FIVE REQUESTS FIRST. The queue's first wanted row is 2008's 09B.pdf -- the
# exact document that answered 403 on 9 September. If it comes back, the
# address was not refusing us and the years below run; if it 403s again, the
# drain records the refusal, exits 2, and the lane stops, which is the answer.
fetch_calendar_archive.py --chamber S --kind calendar --from 2008 --to 2008 --budget 5 --delay 20
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-senate-proving
fetch_calendar_archive.py --chamber S --kind calendar --from 2006 --to 2008 --budget 140 --delay 20
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-senate-2006-2008
fetch_calendar_archive.py --chamber S --kind calendar --from 2003 --to 2005 --budget 170 --delay 20
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-senate-2003-2005
fetch_calendar_archive.py --chamber S --kind calendar --from 2000 --to 2002 --budget 210 --delay 20
watchers/rest.py 1800 after-senate-2000-2002
fetch_calendar_archive.py --chamber S --kind calendar --from 1998 --to 1999 --budget 170 --delay 20
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-senate-1998-1999
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-03
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-03
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-04
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-04
fetch_legislation.py --all --from 1989 --to 2024 --kinds HB,SB,CACR --newest-first --delay 20 --budget 800 --note bills-05
watchers/rest.py 3600 after-bills-05
